@startuml
title Alpaka Redundant Hierarchical Parallelism - Abstraction Hierarchy

skinparam class {
    BackgroundColor LightYellow
    BorderColor Orange
    ArrowColor DarkBlue
    FontColor Black
    FontSize 11
}

skinparam note {
    BackgroundColor LightGreen
    BorderColor Green
    FontColor Black
}

' Parallelism Hierarchy
class Grid {
    + Sequential/Parallel execution
    + Event-based synchronization
    + Multiple queues support
    --
    Backend Mapping:
    • CUDA: Grid of blocks
    • OpenMP: Parallel region  
    • CPU: Thread pool
}

class Block {
    + Independent execution
    + Shared memory access
    + No inter-block sync
    --
    Backend Mapping:
    • CUDA: Thread block
    • OpenMP: Team
    • CPU: Work group
}

class Warp {
    + Lock-step execution
    + SIMD operations
    + Implicit synchronization
    --
    Backend Mapping:
    • CUDA: Warp (32 threads)
    • CPU: SIMD vector width
    • SYCL: Sub-group
}

class Thread {
    + Fine-grained parallelism
    + Block-local barriers
    + Atomic operations
    --
    Backend Mapping:
    • CUDA: Individual thread
    • OpenMP: Thread in team
    • CPU: OS thread
}

class Element {
    + Sequential processing
    + Register optimization
    + Vectorization opportunity
    --
    Backend Mapping:
    • Loop within thread
    • Multiple data per thread
}

' Memory Hierarchy
class GlobalMemory {
    + Accessible by all threads
    + Largest but slowest
    + Host-device transfers
    --
    Properties:
    • High latency
    • High bandwidth
    • Coalescing important
}

class SharedMemory {
    + Block-local memory
    + Fast access
    + Limited size
    --
    Properties:
    • Low latency
    • High bandwidth
    • Explicit management
}

class Registers {
    + Thread-private memory
    + Fastest access
    + Automatic allocation
    --
    Properties:
    • Lowest latency
    • Limited capacity
    • Compiler managed
}

' Abstraction Layers
package "Backend Abstraction" {
    class DeviceAbstraction {
        + Device enumeration
        + Property queries
        + Resource management
    }
    
    class QueueAbstraction {
        + Asynchronous execution
        + Event synchronization
        + Task dependencies
    }
    
    class MemoryAbstraction {
        + Buffer allocation
        + Transfer operations
        + View management
    }
}

' Relationships - Parallelism Hierarchy
Grid ||--o{ Block : "contains"
Block ||--o{ Warp : "organized into"
Warp ||--o{ Thread : "executes"
Thread ||--o{ Element : "processes"

' Relationships - Memory Access
Thread --> GlobalMemory : "reads/writes"
Thread --> Registers : "private access"
Block --> SharedMemory : "shared access"
Warp --> GlobalMemory : "coalesced access"

' Abstraction relationships
Grid --> DeviceAbstraction : "uses"
Grid --> QueueAbstraction : "scheduled by"
Thread --> MemoryAbstraction : "accesses through"

' Synchronization relationships
Grid ..> Grid : "Event sync\nbetween queues"
Thread ..> Thread : "Block barriers\nAtomic ops"
Warp ..> Warp : "Implicit sync\nSIMD operations"

note top of Grid
    **Grid Level**
    - Entire computational problem
    - Can run sequentially in queue
    - Can run parallel across queues
    - Event-based synchronization
end note

note right of Block
    **Block Level**
    - Independent groups of threads
    - Shared memory allocation
    - No synchronization between blocks
    - Scalable across devices
end note

note right of Warp
    **Warp Level**
    - Lock-step execution model
    - Memory coalescing
    - Predicated execution for branches
    - Latency hiding through warps
end note

note bottom of Thread
    **Thread Level**
    - Individual execution context
    - Block-local synchronization
    - Atomic operations support
    - Register allocation
end note

note bottom of Element
    **Element Level**
    - Sequential data processing
    - Register usage optimization
    - Vectorization opportunities
    - Multiple elements per thread
end note

note left of SharedMemory
    **Memory Hierarchy**
    Each level provides different
    access patterns and performance
    characteristics optimized for
    the parallelism level
end note

legend bottom right
  **Connection Legend**
  → uses: Component dependency
  → scheduled by: Queue scheduling
  → accesses through: Memory access path
  ..> : Synchronization relationship
end legend

@enduml